--TEST--
`Core\ObjectMap<K, ?V>::get` answers `null` for a key it does not hold and for a key holding `null`, so `has` is the only member that tells the two apart, and every other member counts the second and not the first — docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 9
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\ObjectMap::has
// The *edge* shape, at the one place § 9's `?V` read gives up information: a
// map's `get` returns `?V`, so with a nullable `V` the absent answer and a
// stored `null` are the same value. That is the documented cost of having no
// `$m[$k]` subscript to throw and no `getOrNull` twin to ask instead (`rule:core-api/shape-rules`
// R5), and what makes it a cost rather than a hole is that `has`, `count`,
// `keys` and `values` all still separate the two. A map that took `null` as a
// deletion — which is how PHP's arrays and half of PHP's `??` idioms read —
// passes every line here that mentions `get` and fails every other one.
final class Tag {
    public function constructor(public readonly string $name) {}
}

Tag $held = new Tag("held");
Tag $nulled = new Tag("nulled");
Tag $absent = new Tag("absent");

Core\ObjectMap<Tag, ?int> $map = new Core\ObjectMap<Tag, ?int>();
$map->set($held, 1);
$map->set($nulled, null);

// The two reads no member can tell apart, side by side. `??` cannot separate
// them either — it is the same `null` — which is the whole of the edge.
echo "get ", $map->get($nulled) ?? -1, " ", $map->get($absent) ?? -1, "\n";
echo "  both null ", (($map->get($nulled) == null) && ($map->get($absent) == null)) ? "y" : "n", "\n";

// Every other member does separate them: a key holding `null` is a key.
echo "has ", $map->has($held) ? "y" : "n", $map->has($nulled) ? "y" : "n",
     $map->has($absent) ? "y" : "n", "\n";
echo "count ", $map->count(), " empty ", $map->isEmpty() ? "y" : "n", "\n";

string $keys = "";
foreach ($map->keys() as Tag $key) {
    $keys = $keys . $key->name . ",";
}
echo "keys ", $keys, Core\Arr::count($map->keys()), "\n";

// `values()` holds the `null` in the slot its key was written into, so the two
// lists stay the same length and stay paired — a map that dropped the entry
// would answer one shorter here and pair the survivors wrongly.
int $nulls = 0;
foreach ($map->values() as ?int $value) {
    if ($value == null) {
        $nulls = $nulls + 1;
    }
}
echo "values ", Core\Arr::count($map->values()), " of which null ", $nulls, "\n";

// A walk yields the `null`-valued key like any other, and its value comes back
// through the same `get` the walk cannot use to tell it apart.
string $walked = "";
foreach ($map as Tag $each) {
    $walked = $walked . $each->name . "=" . (($map->get($each) ?? -1) as string) . ",";
}
echo "walk ", $walked, "\n";

// Writing `null` over a value is a write and not a removal, and writing a
// value back over the `null` is the same write in the other direction: the
// count never moves and the key never leaves its place in the order.
$map->set($held, null);
echo "overwritten ", $map->count(), " ", $map->has($held) ? "y" : "n",
     " ", $map->get($held) ?? -1, "\n";
$map->set($held, 2);
echo "restored ", $map->count(), " ", $map->get($held) ?? -1, "\n";

// Removal is the one thing that does take the key out, and it is the only way
// to make `has` false. Removing the `null`-valued key is a removal like any
// other — the entry was never a hole to begin with.
$map->remove($nulled);
echo "removed ", $map->count(), " ", $map->has($nulled) ? "y" : "n",
     " ", $map->get($nulled) ?? -1, "\n";

// Removing a key that was never held changes nothing, which is the other side
// of the same bound: `remove` is driven by `has`, not by what `get` answered.
$map->remove($absent);
echo "absent removed ", $map->count(), " ", $map->has($absent) ? "y" : "n", "\n";
--EXPECT--
get -1 -1
  both null y
has yyn
count 2 empty n
keys held,nulled,2
values 2 of which null 1
walk held=1,nulled=-1,
overwritten 2 y -1
restored 2 2
removed 1 n -1
absent removed 1 n
